911 was the worst ever terrorist attack  on u.s soil five prisoners in guantanamo  bay are accused of helping kill  thousands of americans twenty years  after the attack they're still waiting  to go on trial catherine harris spoke  with a woman whose fiance was killed in  9 11. melissa long has visited  guantanamo to push for a trial she calls  it a case of justice delayed justice  denied  he was a firefighter we just had a very  different connection than anybody i'd  ever been with before and i truly loved  him melissa long is describing her  fiance brian sweeney who gave an  interview just before 9 11 about landing  his dream job with one of the most  storied firehouses in new york city  rescue one everyone gives us a lot of  respect because they always see us  running into the burning buildings  because everyone else is leaving i see  your face completely light up when you  talk about brian he makes me happy i  would never people like would you have  rather have never met him absolutely not  on 9 11 brian got the call to the world  trade center i personally had a sense  like that first night that he was gone i  just felt this emptiness in my heart  two weeks later the red cross took  melissa to the still smoldering ground  zero they show you the pile and  you could smell it  you know i think it was their way of  telling us that he was gone  it took 76 days for rescue teams to find  brian sweeney they identified him by his  tattoos  in total  343 new york city firefighters were  killed on 9 11. i stood there and i said  god didn't do this people did  melissa sought justice for brian at the  military courts in guantanamo bay cuba  it's not about people who are saying  they're innocent the five 911 suspects  including the self-described architect  of the attacks khalid sheikh mohammed  are stuck in legal limbo i really do  believe they should have their day in  court and i want my day in court and i  want to hear the evidence defense  attorney edward mcmahon jr represented 9  11 al-qaeda suspect walid binatash who  is accused of working directly with  al-qaeda leader osama bin laden what  explains  a delay of two decades the military  courts are unable to handle these trials  in any efficient manner whatsoever  they've never had a complex death  penalty case with multiple defendants in  2009 president obama's effort to bring  the defendants to trial in new york city  failed yet from the very beginning  defense attorneys say there's been a  government secret so closely held that  has paralyzed the court i don't think  the cia would care if this trial never  went forward  before the 9 11 defendants got to  guantanamo the bush administration held  them in c.i.a secret prisons where they  were interrogated using extreme tactics  that critics called torture to this day  the program remains highly classified  did you see evidence that showed your  client at the time had been tortured in  cia custody no i can't answer that  question is it a classification issue  i'm trying to think how to say this i  don't get arrested but i don't believe  the agency itself has ever conceded that  it occurred and certainly have never  conceded that they tortured anybody who  has the power to say enough is enough to  end this  president of the united states my  prediction is that the 9 11 defendants  will die of natural causes in gitmo  before there's a trial  have the families been let down  yeah i mean for sure nobody cares about  the trials there's no political will i  guess you'd say for that to happen and  that's a shame  a spokesman for the military trials  tells cbs news the cases are complex and  they are committed to a fair and just  outcome but offered no timeline the bind  administration wants to close the  guantanamo detention camp scale  catherine thank you she raised some  interesting questions there 20 years  does seem a very long time you know it's  a good interview  the person you're talking to says let me  see how i can say this without getting  arrested yes right and when you don't  have answers you're constantly reminded  of the pain and we need those answers  that is something that president biden  could do and you're right a whole lot  more to that story come
 

